1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,223
OK, Greg, so I finally managed to read the article you mentioned -

2
00:00:06,223 --> 00:00:10,890
the one about the study on gender in physics.

3
00:00:10,890 --> 00:00:15,820
About the study of college students done by Akira Miyake and his team?

4
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:23,020
Yeah. I was interested that the researchers were actually a mix of psychologists and physicists.

5
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:25,840
That's an unusual combination. Yeah.

6
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:32,040
I got a little confused at first about which students the study was based on.

7
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:35,160
They weren't actually majoring in physics

8
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:38,720
they were majoring in what's known as the STEM disciplines.

9
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:43,800
That's science, technology, engineering and...... and math.

10
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:48,670
Yes, but they were all doing physics courses as part of their studies.

11
00:00:48,670 --> 00:00:52,532
That's correct. So as I understood it, Miyake

12
00:00:52,532 --> 00:01:00,257
and co started from the fact that women are underrepresented in introductory physics courses at college,

13
00:01:00,257 --> 00:01:04,280
and also that on average

14
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:09,430
the women who do enrol on these courses perform more poorly than the men.

15
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:12,360
No one really knows why this is the case.

16
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:21,200
Yeah. But what the researchers wanted to find out was basically what they could do about the relatively low level of the women's results.

17
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:23,380
But in order to find a solution

18
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:27,030
they needed to find out more about the nature of the problem.

19
00:01:27,030 --> 00:01:33,445
Right - now let's see if I can remember... it was that in the physics class,

20
00:01:33,445 --> 00:01:41,570
the female students thought the male students all assumed that women weren't any good at physics... was that it?

21
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:47,440
And they thought that the men expected them to get poor results in their tests.

22
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:50,940
That's what the women thought, and that made them nervous

23
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:53,050
so they did get poor results.

24
00:01:53,050 --> 00:01:55,360
But actually they were wrong

25
00:01:55,820 --> 00:02:00,060
No one was making any assumptions about the female students at all.

26
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:03,275
Anyway, what Miyake's team did was quite simple -

27
00:02:03,275 --> 00:02:08,900
getting the students to do some writing before they went into the physics class.

28
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:10,630
What did they call it?

29
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:16,812
Values-affirmation - they had to write an essay focusing on things that were significant to them,

30
00:02:16,812 --> 00:02:20,934
not particularly to do with the subject they were studying,

31
00:02:20,934 --> 00:02:25,880
but more general things like music, or people who mattered to them.

32
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:33,200
Right. So the idea of doing the writing is that this gets the students thinking in a positive way.

33
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:36,694
And putting these thoughts into words can relax them

34
00:02:36,694 --> 00:02:41,740
and help them overcome the psychological factors that lead to poor performance. Yeah.

35
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:53,590
But what the researchers in the study hadn't expected was that this one activity raised the women's physics grades from the C to the B range. A huge change.

36
00:02:53,590 --> 00:02:57,609
Pity it wasn't to an A, but still! No,

37
00:02:57,609 --> 00:03:02,760
but it does suggest that the women were seriously underperforming beforehand

38
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,200
in comparison with the men. Yes.

39
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:09,500
Mind you, Miyake's article left out a lot of details.

40
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:14,270
Like, did the students do the writing just once, or several times?

41
00:03:14,270 --> 00:03:17,860
And had they been told why they were doing the writing?

42
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:19,920
That might have affected the results.

43
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:24,992
You mean, if they know the researchers thought it might help them to improve,

44
00:03:24,992 --> 00:03:27,890
then they'd just try to fulfil that expectation?

45
00:03:27,890 --> 00:03:28,820
Exactly.

46
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:35,675
So anyway, I thought for our project we could do a similar study,

47
00:03:35,675 --> 00:03:42,530
but investigate whether it really was the writing activity that had that result.

48
00:03:42,530 --> 00:03:50,173
Ok. So we could ask them to do a writing task about something completely different...

49
00:03:50,173 --> 00:03:54,250
something more factual? Like a general knowledge topic.

50
00:03:54,250 --> 00:03:59,821
Maybe... or we could have half the students doing a writing task

51
00:03:59,821 --> 00:04:04,000
and half doing something else, like an oral task.

52
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,600
Or even, half do the same writing task as in the original research

53
00:04:10,020 --> 00:04:12,170
and half do a factual writing task.

54
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:17,720
Then we'd see if it really is the topic that made the difference, or something else.

55
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:25,770
That's it. Good. So at our meeting with the supervisor on Monday we can tell him we've decided on our project.

56
00:04:25,770 --> 00:04:28,837
We should have our aims ready by then.

57
00:04:28,837 --> 00:04:33,820
I suppose we need to read the original study. The article's just a summary.

58
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:36,420
And there was another article I read

59
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:40,661
by Smolinsky. It was about her research on how women

60
00:04:40,661 --> 00:04:44,000
and men perform in mixed teams in class

61
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:47,100
compared with single-sex teams and on their own.

62
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:51,596
Let me guess... the women were better at teamwork. That's what I expected,

63
00:04:51,596 --> 00:04:55,940
but actually the men and the women got the same results

64
00:04:56,100 --> 00:04:58,860
whether they were working in teams or on their own.

65
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:02,440
But I guess it's not that relevant to us.

66
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:07,890
What worries me anyway is how we're going to get everything done in the time.

67
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:10,310
We'll be OK now we know what we're doing.

68
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:13,440
Though I'm not clear how we assess

69
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:19,600
whether the students in our experiment actually make any progress or not... No.

70
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,047
We may need some advice on that.

71
00:05:22,047 --> 00:05:26,060
The main thing's to make sure we have the right size sample

72
00:05:26,380 --> 00:05:28,340
not too big or too small.

73
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:33,400
That shouldn't be difficult. Right, what do we need to do next?

74
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:37,220
We could have a look at the timetable for the science classes...

75
00:05:37,220 --> 00:05:42,950
or perhaps we should just make an appointment to see one of the science professors. That's be better.

76
00:05:42,950 --> 00:05:46,840
Great. And we could even get to observe one of the classes.

77
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:47,790
What for?

78
00:05:47,790 --> 00:05:55,500
Well... OK maybe let's just go with your idea. Right, well...
